# Appeals to First Tier Tribunal

AS[Adam Sierant](/adam-sierant/) · Immigration lawyer, IAA-regulatedUpdated 26 July 2026 · 13 min read

### Appeals to the First-tier Tribunal: Challenging a UK Immigration Decision

If the Home Office has refused your UK immigration application, protection claim, [human rights](/uk-immigration/human-rights/) claim, [EU Settlement Scheme](/home-office-tightens-checks-on-eu-citizens-with-pre-settled-status-what-this-means-for-the-right-to-live-in-the-uk-after-brexit/) application, or made a decision to deport you, you may feel that your future in the UK has been taken out of your hands. In many cases, however, a refusal is not necessarily the end of the road. You may have a right of [appeal](/uk-immigration/appeals/) to the First-tier Tribunal, where an independent immigration judge can look at your case again.

An appeal to the First-tier Tribunal is not simply a complaint about an unfair decision. It is a legal process. The Tribunal will consider whether the Home Office decision is wrong in law, wrong on the facts, or incompatible with your rights. A well-prepared [immigration appeal](/uk-deportation-law-case-law-human-rights-public-interest-test/) can make the difference between removal from the UK and being allowed to remain with your family, continue your life, or secure protection.

### What Is the First-tier Tribunal in UK Immigration Appeals?

The First-tier Tribunal, Immigration and Asylum Chamber, is the independent Tribunal that deals with many UK immigration appeals. It is separate from the Home Office. This matters because your case is not decided again by the same department that refused you. Instead, an immigration judge considers the evidence, the Immigration Rules, Home Office policy, relevant case law, and any human rights issues raised by your case.

For many people, this is the first real opportunity to explain their case properly. A refusal letter may misunderstand the evidence, ignore important documents, question credibility unfairly, or apply the wrong legal test. The appeal process allows those issues to be challenged in a structured way.

### When Can You Appeal to the First-tier Tribunal?

You do not automatically get a right of appeal against every UK visa refusal. This is one of the most common misunderstandings in UK immigration law. Some refusals carry a right of appeal, while others may only be challenged by [administrative review](/uk-immigration/appeals/administrative-review-ar/), judicial review, or a fresh application.

You may be able to appeal to the First-tier Tribunal if the Home Office has:

* refused your protection claim, including an asylum or humanitarian protection claim;
* revoked your protection status;
* refused a human rights claim, including many [Article 8](/uk-immigration/human-rights/article-8-immigration-claims-family-life-private-life-and-human-rights-applications-in-the-uk/) ECHR family life or private life claims;
* made certain [deportation decisions](/uk-deportation-lawyers/);
* refused or revoked status under the EU Settlement Scheme;
* varied the length or conditions of your stay under the EU Settlement Scheme;
* made certain decisions under the EEA Regulations where appeal rights still apply;
* revoked or deprived you of [British citizenship](/uk-immigration/british-citizenship/) in circumstances where an appeal right exists.

The first thing to check is always the decision letter. It should tell you whether you have a right of appeal, the deadline, and how to appeal. If the letter is unclear, or if you are unsure whether an appeal is the correct route, you should get advice quickly. Choosing the wrong remedy can waste time and may damage your position.

### Common Types of Immigration Appeals to the First-tier Tribunal

First-tier Tribunal immigration appeals can arise in many different situations. Some cases are highly urgent because the person is at risk of removal. Others are emotionally difficult "}

